{"kind":"data","event":{"type":"tool_call","status":"complete","toolCallId":"01a15341-b80b-701d-bb99-874d247b4e67","toolName":"read_file","args":{"file_path":"/skills/built-in/contracts/references/deploy-contract-tool.md","limit":2000},"result":"... (truncated)\ntract takes an owner / admin address, pass the platform deployer wallet address stated in the live tool description for this field, unless the user specified otherwise — see `./contract-deploy.md` \\\"Owner / Admin Constructor Args\\\".\\n    39\\t- `compiledArtifactPath`: Optional path to the Foundry artifact JSON. Defaults to `/home/user/app/contracts/out/<name>.sol/<name>.json` (Foundry's layout when the source filename matches the contract name). Override only when the source filename differs from the contract name.\\n    40\\t\\n    41\\tThe tool refuses a stale artifact: if `<name>.sol` is newer than the artifact, it returns a \\\"Stale compiled artifact\\\" error instead of deploying. Run `cd /home/user/app && forge build`, then call the tool again. This refusal does not consume the deploy retry.\\n    42\\t\\n    43\\t## Chain support\\n    44\\t\\n    45\\tMode 1 supports the EVM testnets listed under `blockchain` above (Circle SCP-supported chains). Mainnet is intentionally NOT supported — Mode 1 is testnet-only. If the user asks for mainnet, route them to Mode 2, where they deploy from their own wallet (see `./contract-deploy.md` \\\"Network\\\"). Each chain uses its own SCA deployer wallet provisioned by the Circle Wallets Terraform provider; the wallet IDs are injected into the pod as `CIRCLE_DEPLOYER_WALLETS_JSON`, so adding a new chain on the operator side requires no code change here.\\n    46\\t\\n    47\\tIf a deploy fails with an error like \\\"Missing deployer wallet ID for blockchain X\\\" the operator has not provisioned that chain yet — surface the error to the user and offer to pick a different chain.\\n    48\\t\\n    49\\t## Response\\n    50\\t\\n    51\\t```json\\n    52\\t{\\n    53\\t  \\\"status\\\": \\\"success\\\",\\n    54\\t  \\\"contractAddress\\\": \\\"0x...\\\",\\n    55\\t  \\\"txHash\\\": \\\"0x...\\\",\\n    56\\t  \\\"explorerUrl\\\": \\\"https://explorer.testnet.arc.io/address/0x...\\\",\\n    57\\t  \\\"chain\\\": \\\"Arc Testnet\\\",\\n    58\\t  \\\"contractId\\\": \\\"...\\\",\\n    59\\t  \\\"contractMetadataPath\\\": \\\"/home/user/app/contracts/contract-metadata/SimpleToken.json\\\"\\n    60\\t}\\n    61\\t```\\n    62\\t\\n    63\\tThe `chain` and `explorerUrl` fields follow the selected `blockchain` (e.g. `BASE-SEPOLIA` returns `chain: \\\"Base Sepolia\\\"` and an explorer link under `sepolia.basescan.org`). The `network` field written to `contract-metadata/<Name>.json` is the SCP `Blockchain` enum value (e.g. `BASE-SEPOLIA`) so the `contract-integration-tester` subagent can read it back and pick the matching RPC.\\n    64\\t\\n    65\\t## Timing\\n    66\\t\\n    67\\tThe call blocks until the deploy confirms on-chain, so latency is chain-dependent: `ARC-TESTNET` usually confirms in under ~15s and most testnets in under a minute, but Ethereum-settled testnets (`ETH-SEPOLIA`, `BASE-SEPOLIA`) routinely take 2–5 minutes and can exceed the tool's confirmation-wait window. A timeout error is NOT proof the deploy failed — the transaction may still confirm on-chain. The deploy is idempotent on `name` + bytecode, so retrying after a timeout returns the SAME contract address once the original confirms instead of redeploying. Prefer waiting, and on timeout retry rather than reporting failure to the user.\\n    68\\t\\n    69\\t## Retry\\n    70\\t\\n    71\\tIf the call fails, follow the shared workflow in `./contract-deploy.md` \\\"Retry On Failure\\\" (one automatic retry on transient errors, then surface to the user). On a timeout specifically, see \\\"Timing\\\" above — retrying is safe and idempotent.\\n    72\\t\\n    73\\t## Caveat\\n    74\\t\\n    75\\tThe platform deployer wallet's keys are not exposed to the agent, so the user cannot call owner-only functions on the deployed contract from their own wallet. If the contract has owner-gated logic the user wants to drive themselves, recommend Mode 2 (self-deploy — see `./self-deploy.md`, read in full) instead.\"}],\"tool_call_id\":\"toolu_bdrk_01CnxPo9NBDe5dXPaDxTFJAJ\",\"name\":\"read_file\",\"metadata\":{\"versions\":{\"@langchain/core\":\"1.2.12\"}},\"additional_kwargs\":{},\"response_metadata\":{}}}"}}
